This is absolutely very important. You don't want to run out of
holy water. As Derek said in the other thread, a lot of potions
are useful mostly for turning them into water and then blessing
them. (Though there are several that are useful for alchemy,
which can be important -- don't read the next bit if you don't
want spoilers -- especially [rot13 for spoiler] fcrrq naq tnva
raretl sbe urnyvat cbgvbaf, naq cbffvoyl rayvtugrazrag naq
 yrivgngvba sbe tnva yriry, gubhtu vg frrzf gb zr gung gung
vfa'g nf vzcbegnag sbe n aba-pnfgre.)

The unicorn horn definitely works better when blessed. I
always bless my bag of holding, which means everything
weighs 1/4 as much instead of 1/2 as much, but that's partly
because I tend to packrat a lot of my loot around the
dungeon. Perhaps with better stash management that
wouldn't be necessary. On the other hand you have
some heavy armor there, and will eventually be hauling
around a heavy quest artifact, so you might like the
extra carrying capacity even with your gauntlets of
power.

I believe that there's no benefit to blessing amulets, rings,
wands, or the other tools (maybe I'm wrong about the
lamp), except that it provides an extra layer of protection
against having them cursed by spellcasters. And it's just
as easy to uncurse items as it is to bless them in advance.

Anyway, the main point is: Make sure you get more holy
water before you use up your last potion. And try to keep
it in a bag, because it's very sad-making to have it boiled
or shattered by a fire/cold attack.

Another thing to watch out for: It looks as if you only have
MC2 right now. That means, I believe, that werefoo bites
have a 1/3 chance of giving you lycanthropy, and wraith/
wight/vampire attacks have a 1/3 chance of draining a
level; if you can upgrade to MC3 that will go down to 2%.
That might be something to look into before you wade
into any graveyards, where you could get a couple levels
drained in a row.

Good luck! And I'm no super-expert, so don't take my
word as gospel.


